450: \[
451:  W\hookrightarrow Y\times[r_0,1],\qquad
452:  \partial W=F\sqcup(-E_\epsilon).
453: \]
454: 
455: Its projection lies over \(0\leq q\leq\epsilon\) and inside \(J\),
456: by the exact displayed formula.  The trace is used in the product
457: with its height interval.  Topologically \(W\) is a solid torus
458: with an open three-ball removed.  Its meridian on the incoming
459: torus is the source vanishing cycle \(a\).  The explicit handle
460: also fixes the framing and avoids an uncontrolled normal
461: perturbation of the base coordinate.
462: 
463: **Proof: choose the page and embed the graph.** The weighted circle
464: has degree twelve on the regular-binding Milnor function.  In the
465: already chosen unit-corrected coordinate, the page equation is
466: 
467: \[
468:  \arg(q-\epsilon)+2\pi\,12\gamma=\varphi.
469: \]
470: 
471: Along the real path \(0\leq q<\epsilon\), its twelve gamma values
472: are constant.  Choose \(\varphi\) so that all these values miss
473: \(J\), and so that the short arc \(12J\) lies strictly on one side
474: of the page near the binding.  This is possible because of the
475: chosen length of \(J\).  The interior of the actual page \(V\)
476: then misses the entire projected compression, not just \(F\).
477: At the binding the initial torus meets \(\partial V\); pushing the
478: page a small distance to the opposite page-normal side removes this
479: intersection as well.  Denote this pushoff by \(V^+\).  It is
480: isotopic to \(V\) in \(Y\setminus F\) and is disjoint from the
481: projected compression.
482: 
483: On \(\partial V=E_\epsilon\times S^1_\gamma\), define
484: \(f(p,\gamma)=r_0e^{2\pi i(\gamma-g_\epsilon(p))}\).
485: This is the exact inverse angular coordinate of the rotating
486: incoming graph.  Its integral marking is the gamma marking,
487: because \(g_\epsilon\) is a globally defined real-valued function.
488: Extend this to a smooth map
489: \(f:V\to D^2_{r_0}\): on a boundary collar multiply the displayed
490: value by a cutoff that decreases to zero and is flat at zero,
491: then set \(f=0\) on the remaining interior.  Its graph in
492: \(Y\times D^2\) is embedded because its projection to \(Y\) is the
493: original embedded page.  In the outer annulus attach
494: 
495: \[
496:  W\times S^1\longrightarrow Y\times D^2,
497:  \qquad ((y,r),\theta)\longmapsto
498:  (R_\theta y,r e^{2\pi i\theta}).
499: \]
500: 
501: The polar coordinate records both \(r\) and \(\theta\), so this
502: map is an embedding.  Its incoming boundary is exactly the graph
503: of \(f|_{\partial V}\), and its outgoing boundary is \(T_F\).
504: Choose the radial derivative of the graph collar nonzero.  Extend
505: that collar outward and adjust only the outer-annulus collar of
506: the compression to meet it.  In a normal chart of the common
507: binding these are graphs over the same radial collar, so smooth
508: interpolation gives an embedded union.  All this adjustment is
509: arbitrarily small and leaves the graph inside the closed inner
510: disc unchanged.  At \(\theta=0\) it is chosen on the compression
511: side of the page; the fixed opposite pushoff \(V^+\) remains
512: disjoint.  This is the asserted \(X\).  Novikov additivity gives
513: \(\sigma(X)=\sigma(V)\): the added product \(W\times S^1\) has zero
514: signature, as is also seen by reversing its circle factor.
515: 
516: **Proof: the Euler line, including its clutch.** The integral Thom
517: sequence for the proper codimension-three embedding \(X\subset Z\)
518: contains
519: 
520: \[
521:  H^2(Z;\mathbf Z)=0\longrightarrow H^2(Z\setminus X;\mathbf Z)
522:  \longrightarrow H^0(X;\mathbf Z)=\mathbf Z
523:  \longrightarrow H^3(Z;\mathbf Z)=0.
524: \]
525: 
526: It gives exactly one class \(x\) with the chosen meridian value one.
527: The identical calculation for
528: \(T_F\subset Y\times S^1\) identifies
529: \(H^2((Y\times S^1)\setminus T_F;\mathbf Z)=\mathbf Z\)
530: by the same meridian.  The actual delta bundle has that value,
531: so its first Chern class is the restriction of \(x\).  Classification
532: of principal circle bundles by integral first Chern class supplies
533: an isomorphism of these specified bundles.  This is an integral
534: identification on the whole complement; it retains the angular
535: clutch of degree minus twelve, rather than deleting its gauge.
536: 
537: The normal bundle of the graph copy of \(V\) is the sum of its
538: oriented page-normal real line and the real tangent plane of the
539: parameter disc.  The graph derivative gives an upper-triangular
540: identification with this sum, hence a framing.  Take the section
541: pointing toward \(V^+\).  Its pushoff first moves its disc coordinate
542: to zero inside the radius \(r_0\); its \(Y\)-coordinate is off the
543: graph throughout.  It then moves radially from zero to the outer
544: parameter point \(\theta=0\).  In the outer annulus the trace at
545: \(\theta=0\) is exactly \(W\), whose projection misses \(V^+\).
546: This is a homotopy in \(Z\setminus X\) to the pushoff of the actual
547: page in the original parameter-zero exterior.  The restriction of
548: \(x\) along that normal section is therefore
549: 
550: \[
551:  s^*x=c_1(L_\delta|_V)=c_1(M).
552: \]
553: 
554: The last equality is the exact circle-line comparison P3F.10.
555: Boundary collar choices change only its boundary gauge, and their
556: relative square is zero by the relative cup-product formula.
557: This completes the construction and the meridian comparison.
558: **End of proof.**
559: 
560: ## P3K.7. Proved: the real trace computes the actual spin return index
561: 
562: Cap \(X\) along \(F\times S^1\) with \(C=D^3\times S^1\), using
563: the fixed normal framing chosen for the actual conifold replacement.
564: Denote the resulting closed four-manifold by \(\bar X\).  Its cap
565: relative to \(V\) is
566: 
567: \[
568:  H_a=(W\cup_FD^3)\times S^1
569:       =D^2_a\times S^1_b\times S^1_\gamma.
570: \]
571: 
572: The subscripts retain the marked boundary cycles.  This cap kills
573: \(a\).  The usual elliptic cap kills \(\gamma\); their boundary
574: markings are not silently equated.  Both have signature zero.
575: 
576: Delete the normal disc bundle of \(X\) from \(Z\), and glue
577: \((C\times S^2)\times I\) along the resulting boundary collar.
578: The resulting two closed boundary components are the physical
579: positive return family \(P_f\) and the normal sphere bundle
580: \(B=S(\bar\nu)\to\bar X\).  On the outer side this is precisely
581: the replacement of the fixed \(F\times D^3\) by \(D^3\times S^2\),
582: performed in every parameter fibre.  On the inner side it completes
583: the normal sphere bundle over the cap.  We now specify its two
584: boundary orientations and the return convention explicitly.
585: 
586: Let the circle-first quotient convention be
587: \(\Omega_{X_6}=d\delta\wedge\Omega_Y\).  In the fixed normal
588: complex two-plane the weights are \((-1,+1)\).  Conjugating the
589: negative-weight complex coordinate reverses its real orientation;
590: contracting the positive Hopf volume by the circle vector reverses
591: the outward cone convention once more.  The two signs give
592: \(\Omega_Y=\Omega_F\wedge\Omega_{\nu,\mathrm{Hopf}}\), where
593: the meridian sphere has the positive Hopf orientation.  The graph
594: part of \(X\) has the complex page orientation, and the oriented
595: compression has \(\partial X=F\times S^1_\theta\), in that order.
596: Give \(Z=Y\times D^2\) its product orientation.  Since \(\dim Y=5\),
597: the outward boundary orientation is
598: 
599: \[
600:  \Omega_{\partial Z}=-\Omega_Y\wedge d\theta.
601: \]
602: 
603: The inner boundary of the deleted normal disc bundle is oriented
604: \(-\Omega_{\bar X}\wedge\Omega_{S^2}\).  The product cap preserves
605: these signs.  Thus the actual oriented bordism has boundary
606: \(-P_f\sqcup(-B)\), with \(P_f\) oriented by the fibre followed
607: by positive parameter, and \(B\) by the complex page followed
608: by the positive meridian sphere.
609: 
610: Jin's mapping torus \(M_f\) identifies \((z,0)\sim(f(z),1)\).
611: The physical positive family of the return \(f\), which identifies
612: \((z,1)\sim(f(z),0)\), is therefore \(P_f=M_{f^{-1}}\).
613: Characteristic-number additivity under inversion, together with
614: the two boundary signs just computed, gives
615: 
616: \[
617:  (k,\ell,A)(P_f)=-(k,\ell,A)(B),\qquad
618:  (k,\ell,A)_{\mathrm{Jin}}(f)=(k,\ell,A)(B).
619: \]
620: 
621: This records both reversals.  The following integrations on \(B\)
622: therefore compute Jin's invariants of the specified physical
623: positive return \(f\), with the stated positive meridian class.
624: 
625: The Euler line extends over the bordism.  On
626: \(F\times S^1\times S^2\) its Chern class is the meridian generator,
627: by the positive-weight SU(2) trivialization of P3F.7.  It therefore
628: extends over \(D^3\times S^1\times S^2\).  An ambiguity in the
629: boundary bundle isomorphism is a map to \(S^1\); its homotopy class
630: lies in \(H^1(S^2\times S^1\times S^2)=\mathbf Z\), and the
631: restriction from \(H^1(D^3\times S^1\times S^2)\) is an isomorphism.
632: It extends as well.  The bordism is spin: \(Y\times D^2\) is spin,
633: and the two possible spin structures on the gluing boundary differ
634: only on the same \(S^1\) factor, where either extends over the cap.
635: This identifies the actual spin return symbol and Euler line with
636: the inner boundary symbol by spin bordism, with no analytic corner
637: or contraction of exceptional curves.
638: 
639: Write \(e_v\) for the vertical Euler class of \(S(\bar\nu)\), and
640: define \(b\in H^2(\bar X;\mathbf Z)\) by
641: 
642: \[
643:  e_v=2x-\pi^*b.
644: \]
645: 
646: Existence and uniqueness follow from the sphere-bundle Gysin
647: sequence, since both classes have fibre integral two.  Since the
648: normal bundle of the graph page is framed and its chosen section
649: has \(s^*e_v=0\), P3K.6 gives \(b|_V=2c_1(M)\).  On the product
650: cap \(C\), \(b=0\).  Its restriction on the intermediate product
